<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla</h1><p class="st-meta">1882 CE – 1983 CE · capital Basseterre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British colony joining Saint Kitts, Nevis and Anguilla from 1882, an uneasy union that broke apart when Anguilla rebelled in 1967.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>To cut administrative costs after the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> merged the separate presidencies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a>, into a single colony in 1882, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basseterre">Basseterre</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> federal colony. Nevisians and Anguillians resented the loss of their own institutions to the larger island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a> remained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> island, its estates consolidated into a central factory after 1912, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nevis">Nevis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> turned to small farming, fishing and emigration. A sugar strike in 1935 helped launch the labour movement, later led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bradshaw">Robert Bradshaw</a>, whose Labour Party dominated politics. The colony joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a> in 1958 and became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies-associated-states">Associated State</a> with internal self-government in 1967.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Anguillians rejected rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basseterre">Basseterre</a> and declared the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-anguilla">Republic of Anguilla</a> in 1967; <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> intervened in 1969, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> was formally separated in 1980. The remaining islands became independent as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> on 19 September 1983, under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennedy-simmonds">Kennedy Simmonds</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saint-christopher-nevis-anguilla.jpg" alt="Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Tcfc2349 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saint_Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla_(1958-1967).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1882 CE – 1983 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Basseterre</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Bradshaw, Kennedy Simmonds</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism and Methodism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saint Kitts and Nevis, Anguilla</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saint Kitts-Nevis-Anguilla</dd></dl></aside></div>
